Correct document ID, chunk ID, page, section, and metadata
Chunk content validation
Retrieve chunks and compare with original documents
No unexplained missing or corrupted content
Embedding validation
Verify embedding exists and has expected dimensions
Valid embedding for each eligible chunk
Semantic retrieval
Submit known questions and inspect top-K results
Relevant chunks appear near the top
Negative retrieval
Ask unrelated or unsupported questions
Irrelevant chunks are not presented as strong evidence
Updated document testing
Reprocess an updated document
Current content is retrieved, stale content handled correctly
Batch reconciliation
Compare source inventory, processing results, and vector inventory
Every document has an explainable processing state

How to Measure Vector Quality
Use a labelled retrieval dataset.
Example:
￼ Question: "What is the approval process for a project?"
￼ Expected relevant chunks: 4
￼ Retrieved top 5 chunks: 3 relevant, 2 irrelevant
Metric
Calculation
Result
Precision@5
Relevant retrieved / Total retrieved
3/5 = 60\%
Recall@5
Relevant retrieved / Total expected relevant
3/4 = 75\%

￼ Precision tells you whether the returned chunks are relevant.
￼ Recall tells you whether the expected relevant chunks were found.
￼ Also measure MRR (Mean Reciprocal Rank) to determine how highly the first relevant result appears, and nDCG@K when relevance has multiple grades.
Tools Needed
￼ Vector database query interface or SDK
￼ Python for comparison and reconciliation
￼ SQL/BigQuery if metadata or source inventory is stored there
￼ Postman or an equivalent API client if retrieval is exposed through an API
￼ Excel/CSV for the labelled expected-chunk dataset
Group C — RAG Functional & AI Quality Validation
Objective: Confirm that the RAG engine retrieves the correct KG and Vector context and generates answers that are correct, complete, and grounded. This is the most important AI quality testing activity in your scope.
Build the Golden Question Dataset
Start with 100 questions. Use the KG team's approved validation queries as the foundation, but extend them to cover RAG behaviour.
